10) what is Overloading ?
   Method overloading is a feature of Java where, within the same class, we define multiple methods with the same name
   but different parameter lists. The parameter list can differ in number, type, or order of parameters.
   ⭐ In method overloading, method resolution is performed by the compiler at compile time based on the method
      signature. Therefore, method overloading is called compile-time polymorphism, static polymorphism, or early binding.

   a) Automatic promotion in overloading-- while resolving the overloaded methods if the exact method is not available
   then we don't get any compiletime error. it will promote the argument to the next level and check whether the matched
   is available or not.
     byte->short
	            ->int->long->float->double
     char------
   b) while resolving overloaded methods compiler will always give the more precedence to child type argument when
      compared with parent type argument
   c)When overloaded methods have two unrelated reference types at the same level, passing null can result in an
     ambiguous method call because null is compatible with both types and neither type is more specific than the other.
      Ex:-
      void show(String s) {
       System.out.println("String");
      }

    void show(StringBuilder sb) {
    System.out.println("StringBuilder");
    }

	show(null);
    Both methods are applicable because null can be assigned to any reference type:
    null → String
    null → StringBuilder
	➡️ Compile-time error: reference to show is ambiguous.

   d) If overloaded methods have parameters in different orders and we pass arguments that can be promoted to match
	 both methods equally, the compiler cannot determine which method to invoke, resulting in an ambiguous method call.
	Ex:-
	m1(int i, float f)
	m1(float f , int i)

	m1(10, 10.5f)--int float
	m1(10.5f, 10)-- float int
	ma(10,10)-- ambiguous compiletime error

	e) var arg method will get the least precedence if no exact match is available then give a chance to the var-arg.
	Varargs methods have the lowest priority during method overloading. The compiler first tries to find an exact match
	or an applicable method using normal method invocation rules. Only if no applicable fixed-arity method is found,
	the compiler considers the varargs method.

