20) what is Object type Casting?
    Object type casting means converting an object reference from one type to another type within an inheritance
    hierarchy.

    a. Upcasting:- Converting a child-class reference to a parent-class reference.
       class Animal {
           void eat() {
               System.out.println("Eating");
           }
       }

       class Dog extends Animal {
           void bark() {
               System.out.println("Barking");
           }
       }

       Animal a = new Dog();  // Upcasting
       a.eat();
       ---Dog is the child class of Animal.
       ---A Dog object is assigned to an Animal reference.
       ---Upcasting happens implicitly.
       ---Through a, you can directly access only the members available in the Animal reference type. Overridden
          instance methods still use runtime polymorphism.

    b. Converting a parent-class reference back to a child-class reference.
       Ex:-
       Animal a = new Dog();

       Dog d = (Dog) a;  // Downcasting
       d.bark();

       ---We use (Dog) to perform explicit casting.
       ---Down casting is needed to access child-specific members through the child reference.
       ---The actual object must be compatible with the target type; otherwise, Java throws ClassCastException.
21. Explain static control flow?
    Static control flow describes the order in which static variables are initialized and static blocks are executed
    when a class is loaded, before the main() method executes.
    Steps of static control flow
    1. Identification of static members: The JVM identifies static variables and static blocks in the class.
    2. Execution from top to bottom: Static variable assignments and static blocks execute in the order they appear in
       the source code.
    3. Execution of main(): After class initialization completes, the JVM invokes the main() method, if it is the
       application's entry point.

