32. what is Singleton class in Java?
    “A Singleton class is a class that ensures only one instance of that class is created and provides a global access
    point to that instance throughout the application.
    It is useful for shared services such as logging, configuration management, and other resources that should have a
    single shared instance.”
    Ex:-
    class Singleton {
        private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();

        private Singleton() {
            // Prevent object creation from outside
        }

        public static Singleton getInstance() {
            return INSTANCE;
        }
    }
    Usage:
    Singleton s1 = Singleton.getInstance();
    Singleton s2 = Singleton.getInstance();

    System.out.println(s1 == s2); // true

    Ex:-Lazy Singleton with Double-Checked Locking
    public class Logger {

        private static volatile Logger instance;

        private Logger() {
        }

        public static Logger getInstance() {
            if (instance == null) {
                synchronized (Logger.class) {
                    if (instance == null) {
                        instance = new Logger();
                    }
                }
            }
            return instance;
        }
    }

33. Advantage of singleton class?
    1.Global point of access: A Singleton provides a single, shared instance that can be accessed through a static
      method, such as getInstance().
    2.Controlled instance creation: It ensures that only one instance of the class is created, which can be useful for
      shared services such as logging or configuration management.
    3.Potential memory savings: It avoids creating multiple instances of the same shared service. However, memory
      savings depend on the use case; a Singleton is not always more memory-efficient.

34. Disadvantages of Singleton?(Important)
    “Singleton has some disadvantages, such as global state, tight coupling, and difficulty in unit testing. If it
    maintains mutable shared state, we must also handle thread safety. Therefore, I would use Singleton only when a
    single shared instance is genuinely required.”

    a. What is the Singleton design pattern?(Important)
       Ensure only one instance of a class exists and provide a global access point to it.
    b. What is the difference between eager and lazy initialization?(Important)
       Eager creates the instance during class initialization; lazy creates it when first requested.
    c. How do you implement a thread-safe Singleton?(Important)
       Know synchronized, double-checked locking with volatile, and the static inner class approach
    d. Why do we need volatile in double-checked locking?(Important)
       It ensures visibility between threads and prevents(Instructor reordering) unsafe publication caused by reordering.
    e. Can we break a Singleton using Reflection?(Important)
       Yes. Reflection can sometimes access a private constructor and create another instance. An enum Singleton is
       resistant to this kind of attack.
    f. Can serialization break a Singleton?
       Yes, deserialization can create another instance unless you handle it, typically with readResolve(). Enum
       singletons handle serialization specially
    g. Can we clone a Singleton object?
       Cloning can produce another instance unless cloning is prevented or handled appropriately.
    h. How would you implement Singleton using an enum?
       public enum Logger {
           INSTANCE;

           public void log(String message) {
               System.out.println(message);
           }
       }
       Usage: Logger.INSTANCE.log("Hello");
    i. Is a Singleton instance unique across an entire application?
       Is a Singleton instance unique across an entire application?

       Not necessarily. It is generally one instance per class loader for a typical Java Singleton. Multiple JVMs, class
       loaders, or application instances may each have their own instance.

























